<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klaipėda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Memel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Klaipėda, historically called Memel, is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s third largest city and only seaport, at the mouth of the Curonian Lagoon on the Baltic. It grew from a castle founded by the Livonian Order in 1252 and belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> for most of its history. Detached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1920, it was taken by Lithuania in 1923, annexed by Nazi Germany in March 1939 and returned to Lithuania after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klaipeda.jpg" alt="Klaipėda" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mantas Volungevicius · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quite_summer_evening_in_the_port_city_Klaipeda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
